  "prompt": "Feature: in-app customization of the AI prompts, per group, replacing the OPENAI_CUSTOM_PROMPT_DIR setting.\n\nCustom prompts are files under OPENAI_CUSTOM_PROMPT_DIR, global to the instance. Groups should customize each prompt in the app (frontend not graded); the setting goes away, migrated into the database.\n\nExpected:\n1. New table ai_prompt_overrides: id (GUID, primary key), settings_id (GUID, foreign key to ai_provider_settings.id, not null, indexed), name (String, not null, indexed; dot notation such as \"recipes.build-recipe\"), mode (String, not null: \"append\" or \"override\"), content (String, not null), base_prompt_hash (String, nullable), the base timestamps, unique (name, settings_id). AIProviderSettings gets a prompt_overrides relationship (delete-orphan). A group-scoped repository repos.group_ai_prompt_overrides (create fills in settings_id) and schemas AIPromptMode, AIPromptOverrideCreate/Update/Save/Out, AIPromptOut, AIPromptsOut in mealie.schema.group.ai_providers.\n2. Prompt files: compile-source and transcribe-audio move to a \"system\" category (names system.compile-source, system.transcribe-audio); the debug prompt moves to OpenAIService.TESTING_DIR / \"debug.txt\", which ping() reads directly. OpenAIService.get_prompt_names() lists every built-in prompt, user prompts before system ones; get_default_prompt(name) returns the file content, OSError (\"Unable to load prompt\") when missing or empty; is_system_prompt(name); _hash_prompt(content) is the sha256 hex digest.\n3. get_prompt: an \"override\" replaces the default, an \"append\" is added after it. The reserved name \"global\" (GLOBAL_PROMPT_NAME in mealie.services.openai.openai, append only) is group-wide instructions appended to every non-system prompt, before the prompt's own. Appended text follows the default (or override) as: blank line, \"###\", a sentence saying these instructions take priority over conflicting ones above, \"---\", blank line, the instructions joined by blank lines (so the result ends with \"\\nEXTRA\" or \"GLOBAL EXTRA\\n\\nPROMPT EXTRA\"); data injections come after. Other prompts' overrides are ignored; system prompts get no global instructions; with repos.group_id None nothing is applied and repos.group_ai_prompt_overrides.get_all() is never called.\n4. Routes: GET, PUT /{prompt_name}, DELETE /{prompt_name} under /api/groups/ai-providers/prompts (can_manage, else 403) and under /api/admin/groups/{group_id}/ai-providers/prompts (admins only). GET returns {globalInstructions: string or null, prompts: [{name, isSystem, defaultContent, mode or null, content or null, defaultChanged}]}, every built-in prompt. PUT {mode, content} (at most 20000 characters, blank -> 422) creates or replaces the group's customization and returns the same payload; unknown names (\"recipes.does-not-exist\", \"debug\", \"..secrets\") answer 404 on PUT and DELETE; \"global\" with mode override answers 400; the stored base_prompt_hash is the current default's hash, and defaultChanged is true only for an override whose stored hash differs from the current default. DELETE removes it (200 even when none).\n5. The migration creates the table and imports the legacy files: OPENAI_CUSTOM_PROMPT_DIR leaves AppSettings and the migration reads it itself (environment, .env, secrets dir); for every row of ai_provider_settings it inserts \"override\" rows for recipes/build-recipe.txt, parse-recipe-ingredients, parse-recipe-video, resolve-organizers and translate-recipe (names recipes.*), recipes/compile-source.txt (system.compile-source) and general/transcribe-audio.txt (system.transcribe-audio), with the current built-in prompt's hash; blank files, removed prompts and general/debug.txt are skipped. A unit test runs upgrade() on a bare sqlite database holding only ai_provider_settings(id CHAR(32)), so use plain SQL only; without the variable nothing is inserted.\n\nMigration identity: revision id a91720b8a7e7; the tests find the file by that id in its name (template <date>_<id>_<slug>.py).\n\nThis change needs a schema migration (an Alembic revision under the project's migrations) that can be rolled back: its downgrade must restore the previous schema, and the migration must not drop columns or tables or run destructive SQL against existing data. Upgrading, downgrading one step and upgrading again must leave the schema as it was. Chain the revision onto the current head (it becomes the only head) with the project's date-prefixed file name, which must sort after every existing migration: the tests derive the latest revision from the file order.\n\nTests: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/unit_tests/services_tests tests/unit_tests tests/integration_tests/user_group_tests tests/integration_tests/admin_tests` for quick loops; the whole backend suite is `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests` (about 8 minutes).",
